  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\n- Chance of showers and isolated thunderstorms this weekend\n\nQuasi-stationary front with active convection remains stuck over\nIllinois and Indiana. With an 850 mb ridge moving east of the\narea, some higher low-level moisture (increasingly muggy dew \npoints) will drift into southern Lower Michigan this weekend. \nLimited upper-level shortwave forcing, thin CAPE, and weak \nvertical shear will make for generally meager convection today, \noften along zones of temporarily increased surface confluence. \nPWAT will be rather high, so despite the model trends to back away\nfrom heavier mean QPF solutions, rain totals around 0.5 to 1 inch\nremain possible but will be patchy and isolated, and mainly near \nand south of I-96.\n\nAnticipating muggy air with dew points in the lower to mid 70s on\nSunday. Areas of fog are possible in the morning given the moist,\nstill air and the potential for skies to partially clear Saturday\nnight. 850 mb temperatures on Sunday will be a little warmer \nwhile 500 mb heights fall as the upper-level ridge flattens out a\nbit. As a result, CAPE should climb toward 1000 J/kg during the \nday. Scattered thunderstorms may develop in the vicinity of a \ncold front and/or midlevel MCV. Heavier downpours will remain the \nmode in any thunderstorms, and hit-or-miss heavier totals greater \nthan an inch are possible.\n\n- Drier and more comfortable Monday\n\nBehind the cold front, high temperatures Monday may not be much \ncooler, though will be noticeably less humid as air from the \nnorthern plains flows through. A good amount of sunshine is also\nexpected.\n\n- Midweek shower/storm chance\n\nLate Tuesday into Wednesday, somewhat increased low-level \nmoisture returns from the central CONUS, then upper-level heights\nfall and a cold front sags through, associated with an upper-\nlevel low over Hudson Bay and a trough digging into Ontario and \nthe northern Great Lakes. Instability builds and scattered\nthunderstorms may develop with hit-or-miss beneficial rain.",
